WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.070
So, now that you have your claw.md, how

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:04.030
do you actually improve it over time?

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:05.710
Well, one useful command that you can

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.070
try out is this command called /init,

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.230
which it stands for initialize. So, as a

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.030
general rule with all of these markdown

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.750
rules, you don't really need to write

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.390
them by hand. With this command, what it

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.230
will do is it will read your whole

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.550
workspace, and if you don't have a

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.990
claw.md yet, it will actually help you

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.630
draft it. And if you already have a

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.030
claw.md, then what it will basically do

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.230
is give you recommendations on how you

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.310
can improve it and make it better. And

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.109
so, if you type in that command, you can

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:34.550
see it is by default available in your

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.070
Claude Code. And if you just send it

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.150
over, what it will do is it will analyze

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.990
your whole workspace, and if there's

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.990
already a claw.md there, it will just

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.190
suggest improvements on it rather than

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.670
override it. And from here, you can see

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.150
some suggestions as well as an opinion

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.030
by Claude Code based on their internal

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.710
best practices on whether you should

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.830
improve, revise, or change your claw.md.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.030
So, here you can see for me that it is

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.070
recommending to document agents.md,

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.870
which is my bridge to CodeX, which is

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.150
the Open AI app. There are some folders

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.150
that it recommends to reference. And

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:06.990
then, since it also knows that I use a

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.830
dashboard to manage my agents, it is

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.710
recommending that that would be a good

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.070
always-on capability to have in our

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.030
claw.md. And so, from here, you can just

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.550
decide to apply what changes you want or

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.470
for you to have Claude explain it better

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:20.830
to you if in case you're finding it

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.430
necessary to improve your claw.md. But

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.430
again, as a general principle, I would

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.790
suggest that you only do this step when

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:28.950
you think that Claude is getting

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.030
confused from your requests because in

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:32.710
the day-to-day, you actually need to

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.190
just worry less about the claw.md and

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.350
just let it naturally be improved as you

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:41.200
work with Claude Code more and more.
